WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
A configuration parser should be guided by a contract before the parsing branches are written.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:14.920
The contract defines what the program accepts, what it rejects, and how it behaves when input is missing, duplicated, deprecated, or unknown.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:20.620
Without that contract, parser behavior tends to emerge accidentally from control flow.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.580
Start by identifying required and optional settings.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.600
Required settings should be present and valid before the program uses the configuration.

6
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.580
Optional settings need documented defaults.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:41.280
A default is not just a convenience value; it is the behavior the program chooses when no explicit configuration is provided.

8
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.640
Allowed values should be specific.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:54.700
Booleans, enumerations, identifiers, sizes, durations, percentages, paths, and lists each need a rule that matches their use.

10
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.720
Unknown values should not silently become defaults unless that behavior is part of the documented contract.

11
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Duplicate keys and duplicate sections need deterministic behavior.

12
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:12.220
A parser can reject duplicates, use the first value, use the last value, or merge values by rule.

13
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.680
The important part is that the rule is intentional, documented, and covered by tests.

14
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.320
Unknown keys and versioning deserve the same care.

15
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.180
Some systems reject unknown keys to catch mistakes early.

16
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.400
Others allow unknown keys for forward compatibility.

17
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:37.140
Deprecated settings should have visible handling so teams know whether old configuration is still honored, warned about, or rejected.

18
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.260
A good configuration contract makes review and testing straightforward.

19
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:54.240
Reviewers can compare the parser against the schema, and tests can cover required fields, optional fields, defaults, duplicates, unknown keys, allowed values, deprecation, and version behavior.
